---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="mhs — Rough crack"
  description="A rough contiguous crack, meshed two ways: the mollified kernel and a classical triangular dislocation element. Where the two von Mises fields agree, and where the classical one has a singular line that the crack does not."
  current="roughness"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>Rough crack<span class="sub">where mollification earns its keep</span></h1>
    <p>
      A self-affine rough disc, radius 6&nbsp;km, 1044 elements of 0.5&nbsp;km,
      dipping 30&deg; at 20&nbsp;km depth, RMS slope 0.25 (13.9&deg; RMS facet
      tilt). Loaded in a thrust regime to 1.05&times; the first failure, which
      leaves <b>47.3&nbsp;%</b> of it slipping with a peak of 0.27&nbsp;m.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same slip, read back as von Mises stress on one grid, two ways.
      <b>Mollified</b> is this kernel at <i>eps</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1&nbsp;<i>h</i>.
      <b>Classical TDE</b> is cutde on the same triangles with no
      regularisation. The difference state draws only where they part company
      &mdash; <b>1.6&nbsp;%</b> of the volume.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      Away from the crack the two agree to a median <b>1.0001</b>, and to
      1.003 at the 99th percentile. This is not a page about a kernel being
      wrong. It is a page about <i>where</i> a classical element has no value
      to give.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      The box is tight on the crack and entirely below the free surface, so
      every point is inside the body and nothing is masked.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="state"><span>state</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="field"><span>field</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="map"><span>colours</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="crack"><span>crack surface</span></label>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="decades"><span>decades</span></div>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="Three movable orthogonal slices through the von Mises stress around a rough dipping crack, computed with a mollified kernel and with a classical triangular dislocation element, with the crack's own triangulated surface drawn inside the box"></div>
    <div data-bar class="bar"></div>
    <div class="panel slices">
      <span class="lbl">slices</span>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="slice-x"><span>x</span></div>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="slice-y"><span>y</span></div>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="slice-z"><span>z</span></div>
    </div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the difference is</h2>
  <p>
    A collocation scheme only ever asks a kernel for traction at an element&rsquo;s
    own centroid, and that is the one place where the classical finite part
    exists. Approached along its normal, a classical element&rsquo;s own centroid
    is perfectly well behaved: stepping in from 30&nbsp;<i>eps</i> to
    0.003&nbsp;<i>eps</i> the von Mises stress changes by less than a per cent,
    with a log-log slope of <b>+0.002</b> &mdash; flat, and matching the
    mollified kernel to 1&ndash;3&nbsp;%. That is why a classical triangular
    dislocation element assembles a perfectly good interaction matrix on this
    mesh, and on a separate head-to-head over four roughness levels it recovers
    the Eshelby solution marginally <i>better</i> than the mollified kernel does.
  </p>
  <p>
    A stress <b>field</b> is a different question, because it asks for stress
    everywhere rather than at centroids. Two adjacent elements that slip by
    different amounts leave a residual dislocation on the edge they share, and
    its stress diverges. Approaching the midpoint of one interior shared edge of
    this mesh &mdash; two faces 4.6&deg; apart, slipping 0.256 and 0.233&nbsp;m,
    so a 9.9&nbsp;% mismatch:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th><i>d</i>/<i>eps</i></th><th>mollified (GPa)</th>
            <th>classical (GPa)</th><th>ratio</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>0.001</td><td>6.01e-3</td><td>3.95e+0</td><td><b>658</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>0.01</td><td>6.01e-3</td><td>3.95e-1</td><td>65.8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0.1</td><td>6.10e-3</td><td>4.02e-2</td><td>6.59</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>6.87e-3</td><td>7.43e-3</td><td>1.08</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>5.47e-3</td><td>5.50e-3</td><td>1.004</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The classical log-log slope over the inner two decades is
    <b>&minus;0.9963</b>: a clean 1/<i>d</i>. The mollified slope is
    <b>+0.0032</b>. At 0.05&nbsp;m from the edge the classical von Mises stress
    has reached 3.95&nbsp;GPa &mdash; 13&nbsp;% of the shear modulus, from a
    quarter-metre of slip.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The cause is the slip mismatch, not the geometry</b>, and that is settled
    by a control rather than argued: set those two elements&rsquo; slip equal and
    change nothing else, and the singularity disappears outright &mdash; slope
    +0.0003, ratio 1.017. Make the slip uniform over the whole crack and the two
    kernels agree to 1.000 at every distance. The divergent line is not physics.
    It is the discretisation&rsquo;s own residue, and there is one on every
    interior edge where the slip varies: <b>1518 interior edges, 770&nbsp;km of
    spurious singular line against 49&nbsp;km of physical rim</b>, a factor of
    15.7, or 6.8&nbsp;km of singular line per km&sup2; of crack.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mollification removes all of it, because <i>eps</i> is a declared physical
    length rather than a property of the mesh: the jump is smeared over a
    width <i>eps</i>, so a shared edge carries a smooth profile instead of a
    line. The physical crack front &mdash; the rim, where the stress really is
    singular &mdash; is the only one left, and it is regularised to the same
    declared width.
  </p>

  <h2>What this volume does and does not show</h2>
  <p>
    The grid is 0.4&nbsp;km, which is <b>8&nbsp;<i>eps</i></b>. It therefore
    undersamples the singular lines by design, and the ratios it reports are a
    <i>lower bound</i>: the largest classical/mollified ratio anywhere on this
    grid is 6.29, against the 658 above, because no grid point lands closer than
    about half an <i>eps</i> to an edge. Resolving a 1/<i>d</i> line in a volume
    needs spacing well under <i>eps</i> in all three directions, which is a
    million times this cost. What the volume is for is the <i>extent</i> of the
    disagreement, and that it reads honestly: a thin shell on the crack, and
    agreement to one part in ten thousand everywhere else.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    <b>The crack surface is the mesh itself</b>: 1044 facets on 571 shared
    vertices, welded from the same triangles the field was computed from and
    refused unless it reproduces them. The wireframe on it is its own edge set,
    and the <b>1518 interior edges</b> in that picture are the lines this page is
    about &mdash; there is nothing physical at any of them. Translucent, so it
    never hides a slice; turn it off to read the slices alone.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Colour is log<sub>10</sub> of the field and always was &mdash; the payload
    stores log<sub>10</sub> in each byte, one level per 1/253 of the span
    &mdash; so the ticks are whole powers of ten. What the <b>decades</b>
    slider sets is how much of that ramp is shown, counted down from the
    array&rsquo;s own maximum, with both ends saturating. It opens on all
    3.0&nbsp;decades, because a 1/<i>r</i><sup>3</sup> field in a 20&nbsp;km box
    is mostly far field and narrowing the window buys contrast at the crack by
    flattening the rest: 2.5&nbsp;decades puts 28&nbsp;% of the voxels on one
    colour, 2.0&nbsp;decades 70&nbsp;%. The difference state is the magnitude
    of a signed difference, floored four decades below its own maximum &mdash; the state
    arrays quantise at about 1.6&nbsp;% relative, so anything below that floor is
    under the payload&rsquo;s own resolution rather than a measurement, and it is
    drawn as no-data rather than as a colour. See
    <a href={`${base}examples/`}>Examples</a> for the quantisation bound this
    payload is checked against, and <a href={`${base}concepts/`}>Concepts</a>
    for what <i>eps</i> is.
  </p>

  <script>
    import { startViewer } from "../viz/controls";
    startViewer(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL,
                "rough_manifest.json");
  </script>
</Base>
